Today I studied the BJT as an amplifier and went deeper into its operation and application. One of the major applications of a BJT is amplification. In practice, input signals from devices such as microphones, radios, and sensors are usually very small, often only a few millivolts. To process these signals properly, we pass them through an amplifier.

To build a useful amplifier, we use biasing. Biasing means applying a suitable DC voltage or current to establish an operating point, also called the Q-point, on the BJT’s characteristic curve. This Q-point is very important because it determines how linearly and efficiently the amplifier will work. The operating point should be chosen so that the transistor remains in the active region and can handle signal variations without distortion. It is not simply about keeping the operating point as high as possible; instead, it should be placed appropriately for the required signal swing and linearity.

